Introduction
Most Expert Advisors are built to answer one question: buy, sell, or wait. Constant Proportion Portfolio Insurance was never trying to answer that question. It asks something quieter and, in a way, more useful: given how much room is left above a floor you've promised yourself you won't cross, how much should actually be at risk right now? The idea comes out of 1980s portfolio insurance, originally built for pension funds and mutual funds that wanted a shot at equity gains without any real chance of ending up below a guaranteed value. The original theory wasn't written with MetaTrader 5, gold, or M5 charts in mind. Porting it into an EA means being explicit about what carries over and what does not.
The mechanics are almost suspiciously simple once you see them. You pick a floor, the lowest your account is ever allowed to be worth. Everything above that floor is the cushion, and a fixed multiplier turns the cushion into how much exposure the risky asset is allowed to have. When the cushion shrinks, exposure comes down with it. When it grows, exposure grows too. That's really the whole rule. No forecast, no indicator, no opinion about where gold is headed next week — just a running answer to "how much room do we have left."
One thing that rule doesn't do, worth naming up front: it's a long-only sizing model. CPPI decides how much of the risky asset to hold, not whether to go long or short — there's no mechanism here for expressing a bearish view. "No directional forecast" means it never bets on where price goes next, not that it's neutral about direction in its exposure.
What follows is that rule built as three files:
CPPI_Core.mqh
, which holds both the stateless math and the trading engine that acts on it; the EA that wires the engine into an account; and an indicator that shows the same math running on price history alone. I've leaned hard on the code to do the explaining here rather than paragraphs describing what the code does — most functions are shown in full below; a couple of short housekeeping ones aren't, and I say so when they come up.
Fig. 1. The CPPI decision loop
Runs on every tick: read equity, recompute the floor and cushion, size the target notional, then either rebalance the risky position or flatten and lock.
