What CPPI promises, and what changes inside MetaTrader 5
Textbook CPPI splits money between a risky asset and a risk-free bond, and rebalances continuously between them. That bond leg is doing real work: money sitting in it grows at the risk-free rate and, by definition, is never exposed to loss. MetaTrader doesn't give you a bond leg. An account either holds a position or it holds uninvested equity, and uninvested equity just sits there earning nothing unless your broker happens to pay swap on it. So "safe" in this article means one thing only: not being in the market. That's a real simplification and I'd rather say so here than let it hide quietly inside the code. It means the floor holds up as long as flat equity doesn't lose value between rebalances, which is a fair assumption, but it also means nothing is compounding the floor upward the way a real bond leg would over a multi-year horizon.
Continuous rebalancing doesn't survive the trip either. The academic version assumes you can trade instantly, all the time. This EA evaluates once per tick and rebalances only when the gap between target exposure and the open position exceeds a band. On M5 gold, reacting to every tick would mostly pay spread without improving exposure control. I'm not hiding either of these compromises — they're right there in the code: in the
m_rebalance_band_pct
field and in the fact that
CCppiMath
simply has no term for bond yield at all.
The floor, the cushion and the multiplier: four formulas
Four formulas do all the work. The floor
F_t
starts life as a fraction of your starting capital, and can optionally ratchet upward with a fraction of the highest equity the account has ever reached, so a good run actually gets locked in instead of being handed back later. The cushion,
C_t = max(V_t - F_t, 0)
, is the only money the strategy is allowed to risk at all. Multiply the cushion by
m
and cap it against a maximum leverage figure, and you get the target notional — the ceiling exists specifically so an ambitious multiplier can't quietly turn into unbounded leverage. And
1/m
tells you the single-step drop the portfolio can absorb before the floor itself gets breached between rebalances. At m=3 that's 33%. Which is really the point of choosing m in the first place: it's not a knob you tune for a nicer-looking backtest, it's you deciding in advance how much of a gap you can live with.
CCppiMath and the CPPI state contract, in full
Everything from the previous section gets written down exactly once, as plain static functions: numbers go in, a number comes out, and that's the whole contract.
CCppiMath
doesn't know an account exists, doesn't know a symbol exists, and doesn't send a single order. That's deliberate. This separation is what allows the live EA and the price-only indicator to use identical math without sharing account state. Right next to it sit the lifecycle enum and the logging struct, since both are just different views of the same three numbers:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CPPI engine lifecycle state                                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_CPPI_STATE
  {
   CPPI_STATE_ACTIVE,
// cushion positive, exposure tracked and rebalanced normally
CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED,
// cushion hit zero once; flattened and permanently de-risked
CPPI_STATE_ERROR
// last rebalance or flatten attempt failed (trade/market error)
};
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Snapshot of one CPPI decision point, used for logging/plotting   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
struct
SCppiSnapshot
  {
datetime
time;
// timestamp this snapshot was taken
double
equity;
// account equity V_t at snapshot time
double
floor_value;
// floor F_t
double
cushion;
// cushion C_t = max(equity - floor_value, 0)
double
target_notional;
// m * cushion, clipped to max leverage
double
target_lots;
// target_notional converted to a broker-legal lot size
double
actual_lots;
// current open volume on the risky symbol
ENUM_CPPI_STATE   state;
// engine state at snapshot time
};
And here are those four formulas, exactly as described above, just in the form the compiler actually cares about:
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiMath -- pure, stateless floor/cushion/exposure formulas     |
//| Every method here takes plain numbers in and returns a plain     |
//| number out. Nothing here touches the account, the trade server,  |
//| or symbol properties, which is what lets CCppiEngine (live       |
//| trading) and CPPI_Ind.mq5 (price-only visualization) share the   |
//| exact same math without sharing any account state.               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
class
CCppiMath
  {
public
