Init() and Start(): wiring configuration and capturing V0
Init()
doesn't do much, on purpose. It copies inputs into members and sets up
CTrade
, and skips validation entirely because that's the EA's job at
OnInit()
, not the engine's.
Start()
is the function that actually matters. It takes whatever equity the caller hands it as
V0
— the EA reads that from
AccountInfoDouble(ACCOUNT_EQUITY)
in its own
OnInit()
and passes it in;
Start()
itself never touches the account — and keeps it as the number the base floor gets measured against for the rest of the run. It's also, and this is worth sitting with for a second, the only place in the entire engine where a previous
CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED
ever gets cleared. In practice that usually means restarting the EA, but technically any later call to
Start()
— not only a restart — resets the lock.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::Init                                                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CCppiEngine::Init(
const
string
symbol,
const
double
multiplier,
const
double
floor_pct,
const
double
ratchet_pct,
const
double
max_leverage,
const
double
rebalance_band_pct,
const
ulong
magic,
const
uint
deviation_pts,
const
string
comment)
  {
//--- copy every configuration input straight into member state; no validation
//--- happens here on purpose -- the EA's OnInit() validates inputs before this
//--- is ever called, which keeps this constructor-style method simple
m_symbol             = symbol;
   m_multiplier         = multiplier;
   m_floor_pct          = floor_pct;
   m_ratchet_pct        = ratchet_pct;
   m_max_leverage       = max_leverage;
   m_rebalance_band_pct = rebalance_band_pct;
   m_magic              = magic;
   m_deviation_pts      = deviation_pts;
   m_comment            = comment;
//--- runtime state starts empty until Start() is called
m_initial_equity =
0.0
;
   m_peak_equity    =
0.0
;
   m_floor          =
0.0
;
   m_prev_equity    =
0.0
;
   m_prev_cushion   =
0.0
;
   m_state          = CPPI_STATE_ACTIVE;
   m_last_error     =
""
;
//--- every order this engine ever sends carries the same magic number,
//--- slippage tolerance and filling policy, configured once here
m_trade.SetExpertMagicNumber(m_magic);
   m_trade.SetDeviationInPoints(m_deviation_pts);
   m_trade.SetTypeFillingBySymbol(m_symbol);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::Start                                               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CCppiEngine::Start(
const
double
v0)
  {
//--- V0 is the capital base the floor percentage is measured against; the
//--- ratchet also starts from here since nothing has run yet
m_initial_equity = v0;
   m_peak_equity    = v0;
   m_floor          = CCppiMath::Floor(m_initial_equity,m_floor_pct,m_peak_equity,m_ratchet_pct);
   m_prev_equity    = v0;
   m_prev_cushion   = CCppiMath::Cushion(v0,m_floor);
   m_state          = CPPI_STATE_ACTIVE;
   m_last_error     =
""
;
//--- a restart always clears any previous lock -- this is the only place in
//--- the engine where CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED is ever reversed
PrintFormat
(
"CPPI engine started on %s: V0=%.2f floor=%.2f (floor_pct=%.2f, ratchet_pct=%.2f)"
,
               m_symbol,v0,m_floor,m_floor_pct,m_ratchet_pct);
  }
OnNewTick(): the decision made on every single tick
This is the function that actually runs the strategy, tick after tick. It pulls live equity, updates the peak and the floor, works out the cushion, checks for gap risk, checks the cash lock, and only after all that bothers computing a target lot size to compare against what's really open. Worth pointing out while you're reading it: notice how the comments sitting directly in the function body start at column zero, while the ones inside the two nested
if
blocks are indented to match their code. That's not sloppy formatting — it's the comment convention this article follows throughout, and it's easiest to see in a function with this much branching. One simplification worth flagging here: both the target-lot sizing and the drift check use the symbol's Ask price, even though a long position actually exits at Bid. On a tight spread like gold's that's a small bias, not a correctness bug, but it isn't zero either.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::OnNewTick                                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CCppiEngine::OnNewTick(
void
)
  {
//--- pull the account's live equity; this IS the portfolio value V_t in CPPI terms
double
equity =
AccountInfoDouble
(
ACCOUNT_EQUITY
);
if
(equity > m_peak_equity)
      m_peak_equity = equity;
//--- recompute the floor first -- with the ratchet enabled it can only move up
m_floor = CCppiMath::Floor(m_initial_equity,m_floor_pct,m_peak_equity,m_ratchet_pct);
double
cushion = CCppiMath::Cushion(equity,m_floor);
//--- a gap that already breached the modelled tolerance is worth logging
//--- before anything else happens this tick, win or lose
CheckGapRisk(equity,cushion);
//--- cash lock is checked before any rebalancing logic runs at all
if
(CheckCashLock(cushion))
     {
      m_prev_equity  = equity;
      m_prev_cushion = cushion;
//--- true only if the lock actually took hold; a flatten that failed
//--- reports false so the caller can see this step did not complete
return
(m_state == CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED);
     }
//--- once locked this engine stays locked until Start() is called again,
//--- even if the cushion recovers on paper due to floating profit while flat
if
(m_state == CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED)
     {
      m_prev_equity  = equity;
      m_prev_cushion = cushion;
return
(
true
);
     }
double
target_notional = CCppiMath::TargetNotional(cushion,m_multiplier,equity,m_max_leverage);
double
price           =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_ASK
);
double
contract_size   =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_TRADE_CONTRACT_SIZE
);
double
target_lots     = NotionalToLots(target_notional,price,contract_size);
double
current_lots    = CurrentExposureLots();
//--- convert the lot drift back into a fraction of equity so the rebalance
//--- band means the same thing regardless of account size or symbol price
double
drift_notional =
MathAbs
(target_lots - current_lots)*contract_size*price;
double
drift_pct      = (equity >
0.0
) ? drift_notional/equity :
0.0
;
bool
rebalanced =
true
;
if
(drift_pct >= m_rebalance_band_pct)
      rebalanced = Rebalance(target_lots);

   m_prev_equity  = equity;
   m_prev_cushion = cushion;
return
(rebalanced);
  }
From a target notional to a real order
Converting a notional amount into an actual position takes four functions.
CurrentExposureLots()
adds up open volume under this engine's magic number, but only
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
volume.
NotionalToLots()
always rounds down to the broker's legal step, since CPPI's whole safety case rests on never being more exposed than the formula asked for, and anything under the broker's minimum size just gets treated as flat.
Rebalance()
calls
Buy()
to grow the position, but shrinking it goes through
ReduceLongPosition()
instead of a bare
Sell()
.
ReduceLongPosition()
iterates through open positions and partially closes the relevant long tickets by the required volume. A plain
Sell()
nets correctly against the long on a netting account, but on a hedging account it opens a brand-new short sitting next to the long instead of reducing anything — exactly what this function is built to avoid, since the engine can't assume which account type it's running on.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::CurrentExposureLots                                 |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CCppiEngine::CurrentExposureLots(
void
)
  {
double
total_lots =
0.0
;
int
total      =
PositionsTotal
();
//--- long-only design: this loop sums only BUY positions under our magic
//--- number on our symbol. Counting SELL volume here too would be wrong on
//--- a hedging account, where a stray short can sit alongside our long --
//--- and this engine should never mistake that short for reduced exposure
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < total; i++)
     {
ulong
ticket =
PositionGetTicket
(i);
if
(ticket ==
0
)
continue
;
if
(
PositionGetString
(
POSITION_SYMBOL
) != m_symbol)
continue
;
if
(
PositionGetInteger
(
POSITION_MAGIC
) != (
long
)m_magic)
continue
;
if
((
ENUM_POSITION_TYPE
)
PositionGetInteger
(
POSITION_TYPE
) !=
POSITION_TYPE_BUY
)
continue
;
      total_lots +=
PositionGetDouble
(
POSITION_VOLUME
);
     }
return
(total_lots);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::NotionalToLots                                      |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
double
CCppiEngine::NotionalToLots(
const
double
notional,
const
double
price,
const
double
contract_size)
  {
if
(price <=
0.0
|| contract_size <=
0.0
)
return
(
0.0
);
double
min_lot =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_VOLUME_MIN
);
double
max_lot =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_VOLUME_MAX
);
double
step    =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP
);
//--- round DOWN to the nearest legal step -- CPPI's whole safety argument
//--- rests on never being MORE exposed than the formula actually calls for
double
raw_lots = notional/(price*contract_size);
double
lots     =
MathFloor
(raw_lots/step)*step;
   lots =
MathMax
(
0.0
,
MathMin
(lots,max_lot));
//--- a target under the broker's minimum tradable size is not a small
//--- position, it's an untradeable one -- treat it as flat rather than
//--- silently rounding up past what the formula actually asked for
if
(lots >
0.0
&& lots < min_lot)
      lots =
0.0
;
return
(lots);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::Rebalance                                           |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CCppiEngine::Rebalance(
const
double
target_lots)
  {
double
current_lots = CurrentExposureLots();
double
delta        = target_lots - current_lots;
double
min_step     =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_VOLUME_STEP
);
//--- a delta smaller than half a volume step is noise, not a real drift --
//--- acting on it would just generate broker commission for nothing
if
(
MathAbs
(delta) < min_step*
0.5
)
return
(
true
);
bool
ok =
false
;
if
(delta >
0.0
)
     {
//--- growing the risky position: Buy() adds straight on top of
//--- whatever is already open, correctly on both a netting account
//--- and a hedging account. The broker's own margin calculation is
//--- consulted first, so an order the account cannot fund is refused
//--- here with a clear reason instead of being sent and rejected
double
margin_needed =
0.0
;
double
free_margin   =
AccountInfoDouble
(
ACCOUNT_MARGIN_FREE
);
double
ask           =
SymbolInfoDouble
(m_symbol,
SYMBOL_ASK
);
if
(!
OrderCalcMargin
(
ORDER_TYPE_BUY
,m_symbol,delta,ask,margin_needed) ||
         margin_needed > free_margin)
        {
         m_last_error =
"Rebalance skipped: margin check failed (required "
+
DoubleToString
(margin_needed,
2
)+
", free "
+
DoubleToString
(free_margin,
2
)+
")"
;
         m_state = CPPI_STATE_ERROR;
Print
(m_last_error);
return
(
false
);
        }
      ok = m_trade.Buy(delta,m_symbol,
0.0
,
0.0
,
0.0
,m_comment);
     }
else
{
//--- shrinking it: close the requested volume directly off our own
//--- known long ticket(s) rather than sending a bare Sell(). A bare
//--- Sell() nets correctly on a netting account but on a hedging
//--- account it opens a brand-new short next to the long instead of
//--- reducing anything, which this engine can never allow
ok = ReduceLongPosition(-delta);
     }
if
(!ok)
     {
      m_last_error =
"Rebalance order failed: "
+
IntegerToString
(m_trade.ResultRetcode())+
" "
+m_trade.ResultRetcodeDescription();
      m_state = CPPI_STATE_ERROR;
Print
(m_last_error);
     }
else
{
if
(m_state == CPPI_STATE_ERROR)
         m_state = CPPI_STATE_ACTIVE;
// a clean fill clears a previous error state
}
return
(ok);
  }
