Cash lock, gap risk, and reading out a snapshot
CheckCashLock()
only ever goes one direction. Once the cushion is depleted, everything gets flattened and the state flips to
CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED
, and from then on
OnNewTick()
refuses to re-lever even if the cushion technically recovers. That isn't an oversight being patched over — it's the textbook CPPI weakness, and this engine lets it happen instead of quietly working around it. Two details matter in practice. First, "depleted" is 0.01% of
V0
rather than a fixed dollar figure, so the rule means the same thing on a $500 account and on a $500,000 one. Second, the lock is declared only after
FlattenAll()
reports that every position under this engine's magic number is really closed.
FlattenAll()
isn't reproduced here; it walks the open positions backwards, keeps going past a stuck close, and returns false if any ticket survived. In that case the engine drops to
CPPI_STATE_ERROR
, skips rebalancing for the tick, returns false to the caller, and tries the flatten again on the next tick, so a failed close can never leave live exposure sitting behind a state that says it is gone.
CheckGapRisk()
, by contrast, doesn't act on anything at all — it only logs, and it logs in two tiers. The breach tier is the literal model failure: at exposure = m×cushion, a fall of more than
1/m
in the risky asset costs more than the whole previous cushion, so a tick whose equity drop exceeds that cushion is reported as
CPPI GAP BREACH
. The warning tier fires earlier, once the drop passes just
1/m
of the previous cushion, and is reported as
CPPI GAP RISK
. That earlier trigger is a deliberate safety margin, not an exact measurement. Neither tier is exact: both read whole-account equity rather than this symbol's own price, and a capped or lot-rounded exposure moves the true breach point away from exactly
1/m
.
GetSnapshot()
is the boring one: every field, floor included, is recomputed from live account and symbol data on each call, taking the larger of the stored peak and current equity without writing anything back into the engine. A snapshot therefore never depends on
OnNewTick()
having run first.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::CheckCashLock                                       |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CCppiEngine::CheckCashLock(
const
double
cushion)
  {
//--- "depleted" is a fraction of V0 rather than a fixed dollar amount, so the
//--- same rule means the same thing on a small account and on a large one
const
double
eps = m_initial_equity*
0.0001
;
// cushion at or below 0.01% of V0 counts as depleted
if
(cushion > eps)
return
(
false
);
if
(m_state == CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED)
return
(
true
);
// already locked from an earlier tick, nothing new to do
//--- first time the cushion has been seen at zero: try to flatten everything.
//--- The lock is declared only once every position is really closed. If any
//--- close fails the engine goes to ERROR instead, skips rebalancing this
//--- tick, and tries the flatten again on the next one
if
(!FlattenAll(
"cushion depleted - entering cash lock"
))
     {
      m_state      = CPPI_STATE_ERROR;
      m_last_error =
"Cash lock flatten incomplete ("
+m_last_error+
"), retrying on the next tick"
;
Print
(m_last_error);
return
(
true
);
     }

   m_state = CPPI_STATE_CASH_LOCKED;
PrintFormat
(
"CPPI engine on %s entered CASH_LOCKED (cushion=%.2f)"
,m_symbol,cushion);
return
(
true
);
  }
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CCppiEngine::CheckGapRisk                                        |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CCppiEngine::CheckGapRisk(
const
double
equity,
const
double
cushion)
  {
//--- nothing to compare against on the very first tick after Start(), or
//--- when the previous tick had no cushion left to lose
if
(m_prev_equity <=
0.0
|| m_prev_cushion <=
0.0
)
return
;
double
tolerance = CCppiMath::MaxSingleStepDrawdown(m_multiplier);
double
drop      = m_prev_equity - equity;
//--- two tiers, both log-only. The breach tier is the literal model failure:
//--- at exposure = m*cushion a risky-asset fall of more than 1/m costs more
//--- than the whole previous cushion, i.e. drop > m_prev_cushion. The warning
//--- tier fires earlier, at just 1/m of that cushion, as a conservative margin.
//--- Both read whole-account equity rather than this symbol's own price, and
//--- a capped or lot-rounded exposure moves the true breach point away from
//--- exactly 1/m, so treat them as indicators, not exact measurements
if
(drop > tolerance*m_prev_cushion)
     {
PrintFormat
(
"CPPI GAP RISK on %s: equity dropped %.2f in one tick, exceeding the "
+
"%.0f%% single-step tolerance on a %.2f cushion"
,
                  m_symbol,drop,tolerance*
100.0
,m_prev_cushion);
     }
if
(drop > m_prev_cushion)
     {
PrintFormat
(
"CPPI GAP BREACH on %s: equity dropped %.2f in one tick, more than "
+
"the entire previous cushion of %.2f"
,m_symbol,drop,m_prev_cushion);
     }
  }
