The indicator: an honest, price-only reference simulation
There's a real limitation here and I'd rather name it than quietly work around it. An indicator can see price history. It cannot see the EA's actual account equity history, because MetaTrader simply doesn't store equity as a per-bar timeseries the way it stores OHLC. So
CPPI_Ind.mq5
makes no attempt to redraw the live EA's real curve — that would be pretending to know something it can't. Instead it runs its own hypothetical simulation: starting from
InpInitialCapital
, growing a risky sleeve using the chart symbol's own bar-to-bar returns, and rebalancing with the exact same
CCppiMath
functions the EA uses. Its own file header says this outright, which felt more honest than leaving it implied. And because it's a simulation rather than a replay of real trading, it also skips everything that makes the EA's own numbers messier: spread, commission, slippage, the broker's lot step and minimum lot, any trade that could fail, margin, and the tick-by-tick path within a bar. None of that is modeled here, on purpose — this indicator answers "what would this allocation rule have done to this price series," nothing more. That makes it fine for illustrating the mathematics and limited as a check on Tester or live behavior:
